<html><head><title>How</title>
<meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><h3 style="margin-top:14.05pt;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:14.05pt;
margin-left:0cm"><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;
font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Aptos;mso-bidi-font-family:
Aptos;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">HOW</span></b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB"><o:p></o:p></span></h3>

<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:
12.0pt;margin-left:0cm"><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:
116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Aptos;mso-bidi-font-family:
Aptos;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">Paths to Transdisciplinary Education for
Researchers</span></b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:
116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB"><o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:
12.0pt;margin-left:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:
116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Aptos;mso-bidi-font-family:
Aptos;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">Researchers can primarily participate in
transdisciplinary education in two ways: as initiators or by joining an
existing project.</span><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:
116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB"><o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:
12.0pt;margin-left:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:
116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Aptos;mso-bidi-font-family:
Aptos;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">Much transdisciplinary education arises from
enthusiastic researchers—sometimes, but not always, also working as
teachers—seeking collaboration with educational and societal partners. These
researchers act as pioneers: they make a plan, find like-minded enthusiasts,
and try to gain support within their institution.</span><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;
mso-ansi-language:EN-GB"><o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:
12.0pt;margin-left:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:
116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Aptos;mso-bidi-font-family:
Aptos;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">With the growth of transdisciplinary projects
within higher education institutions, researchers can also join existing
initiatives. Institution-wide events or national conferences are excellent
opportunities to connect with other innovators. Network organizations like
Comenius (for education in the Netherlands) and social media platforms like
LinkedIn offer inspiration and networking opportunities.</span><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;
mso-ansi-language:EN-GB"><o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:
12.0pt;margin-left:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:
116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Aptos;mso-bidi-font-family:
Aptos;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">Societal partners also approach academic
institutions with project proposals. Publicly expressing your interest in
transdisciplinary projects increases the chance of getting connected to
collaborations.<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:
12.0pt;margin-left:0cm"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:
116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Aptos;mso-bidi-font-family:
Aptos;mso-ansi-language:EN-US">During the TRAIL, you will learn about the
different phases of transdisciplinary education, whether you are pioneering or
joining an existing initiative. The first phase involves setting up the team
and collaboration. The next phase focuses on developing the project. Then comes
the phase of participation and creating outcomes. The final phase is about
evaluation, with a view to new challenges.</span><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;
mso-ansi-language:EN-GB"><o:p></o:p></span></p></body></html>